Chestnut Flour Chocolate Chunk Cookies
Soft, a little fudgy, and very chestnut-y.
Makes About 18–20 cookies

Ingredients

Dry ingredients
1 cup (120 g) chestnut flour
3/4 cup (90 g) cake flour
1/4 cup (20 g) unsweetened cocoa powder
1/2 tsp baking powder
1/4 tsp baking soda
1/2 tsp fine salt

Wet ingredients
1/2 cup (113 g) unsalted butter, softened to room temp
1/2 cup (100–110 g) packed light brown sugar
1/3 cup (65–70 g) granulated sugar
1 large egg (room temp if possible)
1½ tsp vanilla extract

Mix-ins
1–1¼ cups (150–180 g) dark or semisweet chocolate chunks or chips
Optional: 1/2 cup (50 g) chopped toasted hazelnuts or walnuts
Optional: flaky salt for sprinkling on top

Instructions
Prep the oven & pan
Heat oven to 350°F / 175°C.
Line 2 baking sheets with parchment paper.

Whisk dry ingredients
In a medium bowl, whisk together:
chestnut flour, cake flour, cocoa powder, baking powder, baking soda, and salt.

Set aside.

Cream butter & sugars

In a large bowl, beat softened butter with brown sugar and granulated sugar
(hand mixer or stand mixer) for 2–3 minutes, until light and slightly fluffy.

Add egg & vanilla

Beat in the egg until fully combined, then mix in the vanilla.
Scrape down the sides of the bowl.

Combine wet and dry
Add the dry ingredients to the butter mixture.

Mix on low or stir with a spatula just until no dry spots remain.
The dough will be soft and a bit sticky (chestnut flour does that—normal!).
Fold in chocolate (and nuts if using)
Fold in chocolate chunks and any nuts.

Cover the bowl and chill the dough 30–60 minutes to make it easier to scoop and prevent overspreading.

Scoop & bake
Scoop dough into balls about 1½ Tbsp each (a small cookie scoop works well).
Space them at least 2 inches apart on the baking sheets.
Bake for 10–12 minutes, until: edges are just set, centers still look slightly soft and a bit puffy.

If using flaky salt, sprinkle lightly on top as soon as they come out of the oven.


Cool
Let cookies cool on the tray for 5 minutes, then transfer to a rack to cool completely.
They’ll firm up as they cool but stay tender thanks to the chestnut flour and cake flour combo.

Notes & Tweaks

More chestnut flavor:
Swap 2 Tbsp of cake flour for an extra 2 Tbsp of chestnut flour.

Chewier texture:
Use all brown sugar (about 3/4 cup / 150 g) and skip the white sugar.

Less chocolatey:
Reduce cocoa powder to 2 Tbsp and add 2 Tbsp extra cake flour.

